{
  "_schema_notes": "One flat, cross-position ranking. Pitchers use a five-part weighted composite (formula.pitcherWeights) benchmarked against era-specific league averages (formula.eraBaselines): eraScore = (leagueEra / era) * 100; whipScore = (leagueWhip / whip) * 100, where whip = (bb + hitsAllowed) / ip; k9Score = (k9 / leagueK9) * 100, where k9 = so * 9 / ip; winsIndex = (wins / (wins + losses)) * 100; savesIndex = (saves / leagueSaves) * 100. Composite = 0.25*eraScore + 0.25*whipScore + 0.20*k9Score + 0.20*winsIndex + 0.10*savesIndex. Hitters use a four-part weighted composite (formula.hitterWeights, 0.35/0.35/0.15/0.15) benchmarked the same way: hitScore = (obp / leagueObp) * 100; powerScore = (iso / leagueIso) * 100, where iso = slg - avg; fieldScore = (fieldingPct / positionalFieldingBaseline) * 100; runScore = 100 + ((netSbValuePerPa - leagueNetSbValuePerPa) * formula.sbValueScale), where netSbValuePerPa = (sb*sbRunValues.sb + cs*sbRunValues.cs) / pa -- Run Score is a DIFFERENCE from league, not a ratio (see caveat 2 below for why). Composite = hitWeight*hitScore + powerWeight*powerScore + fieldWeight*fieldScore + runWeight*runScore. League baselines (formula.eraBaselines) and positional fielding baselines (formula.fieldingBaselines) are both computed at load time by blending period buckets across the exact years in a player's 'years' string, weighted by how many of each bucket's seasons overlap the player's career -- a career spanning two eras/regimes gets a blended baseline instead of one arbitrary constant. IMPORTANT CAVEATS: (1) eraBaselines/fieldingBaselines are researched approximations built from published era-level trends, not exact year-by-year league totals from a play-by-play database -- treat as directionally right, not authoritative to the decimal. (2) League-average net SB value per PA is naturally very close to zero in most eras (the running game roughly break-evens in aggregate), so an early version of this formula divided by it directly and produced wild swings (Babe Ruth's composite went to roughly -7800). Run Score was changed to a difference-from-league (scaled by formula.sbValueScale = 15000, chosen so a typical career-length SB/CS differential of about +/-0.003 net value per PA swings Run Score by roughly +/-45 points around the 100 baseline) specifically to avoid that instability. (3) 'years' is treated as a continuous span for era-blending purposes even for players with mid-career gaps (military service, injury, etc.) -- e.g. Ted Williams' 1943-1945 and 1952-1953 service years are counted as part of his career span. Regenerate by pulling real career avg/hits/hr/obp/slg/sb/cs/pa/fieldingPct for hitters, or wins/losses/era/so/ip/bb/hitsAllowed/saves for pitchers, keeping these exact field names.",
